{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothFinalAssembly","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.RothFinalAssembly","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothIntegerChain","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothNormalisation","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothSiegelBound","lean:module:Mathlib.RingTheory.Localization.Integral","lean:module:Mathlib.RingTheory.Algebraic.Integral","lean:module:Mathlib.FieldTheory.IntermediateField.Adjoin.Basic"],"declarations":21,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.finite_approximations_of_isAlgebraic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.finite_approximations_of_isAlgebraic","kind":"theorem","line":958,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothFinalAssembly.lean#L958-L983","doc":"**Roth's theorem, classical form.**  For a real algebraic `x` and any `ε > 0`, the inequality\n`|x − p/q| ≤ q^{−(2+ε)}` has only **finitely many** rational solutions.\n\nThe step from `not_approximable_of_isAlgebraic` is not bookkeeping: \"no family of unbounded\ndenominator\" and \"finitely many solutions\" differ by the discreteness of `ℚ`, which is\n`finite_den_le`.  What makes them meet is that a solution is automatically within `1` of `x`,\nbecause `q^{−(2+ε)} ≤ 1` — so an infinite solution set with bounded denominators would sit inside a\nfinite set. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.finite_den_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.finite_den_le","kind":"theorem","line":917,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothFinalAssembly.lean#L917-L956","doc":"**Rationals of bounded denominator near a fixed real are finitely many.**  The `num`/`den` pair\ndetermines the rational, the denominator is bounded by hypothesis, and the numerator is bounded\nbecause `|κ| ≤ |x| + 1` forces `|num| ≤ (|x|+1)·den`.\n\nThis is the discreteness of `ℚ`, and it is exactly what separates \"no family of unbounded\ndenominator\" from \"finitely many solutions\".  Nothing earlier in the arc supplies it — every\nestimate so far is about *how good* an approximation is, none about *how many* there are. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.not_approximable_of_isAlgebraic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.not_approximable_of_isAlgebraic","kind":"theorem","line":868,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothFinalAssembly.lean#L868-L915","doc":"**Roth's theorem, for every real algebraic number.**\n\n`|x − p/q| ≤ q^{−(2+ε)}` has no solutions of unbounded denominator, for any real algebraic `x` and\nany `ε > 0`.\n\nEverything analytic is already done — `not_approximable_den_of_nsmul_eq` handles both exponent\nconversions.  What is left is **construction**, and it is the last step of the arc:\n\n* `IsFractionRing.isAlgebraic_iff ℤ ℚ ℝ` turns `IsAlgebraic ℚ x` into `IsAlgebraic ℤ x` by clearing\n  denominators, and `exists_integral_multiple` then produces a non-zero `c : ℤ` with `c • x`\n  integral.  The sign is discarded by passing to `c.natAbs`, since integrality survives negation.\n* `ℚ⟮y⟯ ⊆ ℝ` for `y = |c|·x` is a number field: finite-dimensional because `y` is integral over `ℚ`,\n  and of characteristic zero because it sits inside `ℝ`.\n* The real embedding is the inclusion, and it is injective because it is a ring map out of a field —\n  which is also what lets `IsIntegral.tower_bot` carry integrality *down* from `ℝ` to `ℚ⟮y⟯`, the\n  one step that does not go the obvious way.\n\nNote that `y` need not be primitive for anything: `ℚ⟮y⟯` is used only as *some* number field\ncontaining `y`, never as `ℚ(x)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.not_approximable_den_of_nsmul_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.not_approximable_den_of_nsmul_eq","kind":"theorem","line":846,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothFinalAssembly.lean#L846-L860","doc":"**Roth in the classical denominator form**, for anything with an integral multiple in `𝓞 K`.\n\nComposes the two conversions: `approximable_height_of_den` moves from denominators to heights at\nthe cost of an arbitrarily small slice of the exponent, and `not_approximable_of_nsmul_eq` moves\nfrom `x` to `n·x` at the cost of another.  Both slices are free because the conclusion quantifies\nover all `ε > 0`, so each is taken at `ε/2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.approximable_height_of_den","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.approximable_height_of_den","kind":"theorem","line":778,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothFinalAssembly.lean#L778-L844","doc":"**Height form from denominator form.**  The classical statement bounds `|x − p/q|` by a power of\nthe *denominator*; everything above is stated in terms of the *height* `max(|p|, q)`.  The two\ndiffer, and the difference costs a slice of the exponent.\n\n`H ≥ q` always, so `q^{-(2+ε)} ≥ H^{-(2+ε)}` — the height form is the *stronger* hypothesis, and it\ndoes not follow from the denominator form for free.  What makes it follow is that a rational close\nto a bounded `x` has a bounded numerator: `|p| ≤ (B+1)q`, hence `H ≤ (B+1)q`, and then\n`q^{-(2+ε)} ≤ H^{-(2+δ)}` once `(B+1)^{2+δ} ≤ q^{ε−δ}`.  True for large `q`, and the hypothesis\nsupplies unboundedly many.\n\nThis is flagged as **R0** in `docs/audits/2026-08-30-roth-arc-ROAST.md`: the gap is benign but it\nhad never been written down, and \"benign and unwritten\" is what this repo's taxonomy exists to\ncatch. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.not_approximable_of_nsmul_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.not_approximable_of_nsmul_eq","kind":"theorem","line":758,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothFinalAssembly.lean#L758-L776","doc":"**Roth for anything with an integral multiple in `𝓞 K`.**  If `n·x = ψ α₀` for some `n > 0` and\nsome algebraic integer `α₀`, then `x` is not approximable to order `2 + δ` for any `δ > 0`.\n\nThis is what `approximable_nsmul` is *for*, and it is here because a lemma nobody calls is not a\ncompleted step.  The scaling costs an exponent, so the proof applies Roth at `δ/2` rather than at\n`δ` — which is free, because the conclusion quantifies over all `δ > 0`.\n\nThe remaining distance to \"every real algebraic number\" is now purely the construction of `K`, `ψ`\nand `α₀` from `IsAlgebraic ℚ x`: `exists_integral_multiple` gives the `n`, `ℚ(n·x) ⊆ ℝ` gives `K`\nand `ψ`, and integrality gives `α₀ ∈ 𝓞 K`.  No further estimate is involved. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.approximable_nsmul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.approximable_nsmul","kind":"theorem","line":689,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothFinalAssembly.lean#L689-L756","doc":"**Approximability transports along an integer multiple, at a strictly smaller exponent.**  If\n`x` admits rational approximations of unbounded height to order `2 + δ`, then `n·x` admits them to\norder `2 + δ'` for every `δ' < δ`.\n\nThe whole argument is linear once written in logarithms, which is why it is written that way here:\nthe condition is `(3 + δ')·log n ≤ (δ − δ')·log H`, so the threshold fed to the hypothesis is\n`exp((3+δ')·log n / (δ−δ'))`, and both height bounds enter as `log H(nκ) ≤ log n + log H(κ)` and\n`H(κ) ≤ n·H(nκ)`.  Nothing here needs the monotonicity of `x ↦ x^c` for negative `c`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.one_le_height","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.one_le_height","kind":"theorem","line":685,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothFinalAssembly.lean#L685-L687","doc":"**Every rational has height at least 1**, because its denominator does. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.height_le_height_nsmul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.height_le_height_nsmul","kind":"theorem","line":659,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothFinalAssembly.lean#L659-L683","doc":"**`H(κ) ≤ n·H(nκ)`**, from the representation `κ = (nκ).num / (n·(nκ).den)`.  This is the half\nthat keeps the transported heights unbounded. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.height_nsmul_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.height_nsmul_le","kind":"theorem","line":641,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothFinalAssembly.lean#L641-L657","doc":"**`H(nκ) ≤ n·H(κ)`**, from the representation `nκ = (n·κ.num)/κ.den`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.height_divInt_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.height_divInt_le","kind":"theorem","line":626,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothFinalAssembly.lean#L626-L639","doc":"**The height of any representation bounds the height.**  For `b ≠ 0`, reducing `a/b` divides the\nnumerator and the denominator, so `H(a/b) ≤ max(|a|, |b|)` whether or not `a/b` was in lowest\nterms.  Both scaling bounds are this lemma at a different representation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.not_approximable_of_algebraic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.not_approximable_of_algebraic","kind":"theorem","line":543,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothFinalAssembly.lean#L543-L598","doc":"**Step 5 — Roth's theorem, in the \"not approximable to order `2 + δ`\" form.**\n\nFor a real embedding `ψ` of a number field and an algebraic integer `α₀`, there is **no** sequence\nof rationals of unbounded height approximating `ψ α₀` to within `H^{-(2+δ)}`.\n\nEverything the contradiction needs is now manufactured here rather than assumed:\n\n* the constants — `B` above the house of `α₀` and above `|ψ α₀| + 1`, `C₀` above `c K`, `W` above\n  `4B` — are ceilings, and exist because those are three real numbers;\n* the parameters come from `exists_roth_parameters`, which is where the non-vacuity lives;\n* the supply `S` is *defined* as the set of heights that admit a good approximation, so `app` holds\n  by unfolding and `hS` is exactly the hypothesis being refuted.\n\n⚠ **Scope.**  This is Roth for `ψ α₀` with `α₀` an algebraic *integer* of `K`.  Extending to every\nreal algebraic number needs one more step — clearing denominators, so that an arbitrary algebraic\n`x` is `ψ α₀ / n` for some `α₀ ∈ 𝓞 K` — and the approximation exponent has to be carried across\nthat scaling.  That step is not done here, and the statement below says only what it proves. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_roth_parameters","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_roth_parameters","kind":"theorem","line":404,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothFinalAssembly.lean#L404-L534","doc":"**The parameters exist.**  Every hypothesis of `roth_contradiction_selected` *except the supply\n`app`* is satisfiable, simultaneously, for any `δ > 0` and any `K`, `B`, `C₀`, `W`.\n\nThis is the non-vacuity check, and it is not optional.  `roth_contradiction_selected` derives\n`False` from a list of conditions; a contradictory list would prove it just as well and would look\nexactly the same from outside.  `app` is deliberately excluded — it *is* the negation of Roth's\ntheorem, so no witness for it can exist and asking for one would be asking to refute the result.\n\nThe order of choice is forced, and every step is Archimedean:\n\n1. `t` from `exists_slack_bound` — the common bound on all four slacks.\n2. `s : ℚ` below both `t` and `1/2`; it serves as `ε`, as `θ`, and as `ν/m`.\n3. `k` past `1/s`, so the normalisation slack `1/k` is below `s`.\n4. `m` past `[K:ℚ]/(2s²)` — the *doubled* unknowns-beat-conditions threshold.\n5. `ν := s·m`.\n6. `q` past `rothConstant(m)^(2^{m−1}) / ν^(2^{m−1})` at `p = 1`, then `F` from `hfloorExp`.\n7. `T` past `exp((2 + log C₀W + log 4B + s·log B)/s)` and past `C₀^{mq}·W^{2m²q}`.\n\n`ν` is the step worth naming.  `hgap` bounds it **below** and `hfin` bounds it **above**, so `ν`\ncannot be made small; only `ν/m` can, which is why `hfin_of_slack`'s division by `m` is not\ncosmetic.  Taking `ν = s·m` pins `ν/m` at `s` while `hgap`'s lower bound falls away as `q` grows —\nthe two constraints are compatible only in that order. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_slack_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_slack_bound","kind":"theorem","line":386,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothFinalAssembly.lean#L386-L396","doc":"**Such a `t` exists.**  Pure Archimedes: the threshold `δ/(16+5δ)` is positive. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.hfin_of_slack","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.hfin_of_slack","kind":"theorem","line":371,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothFinalAssembly.lean#L371-L384","doc":"**The slack form is `hfin`.**  Dividing `roth_contradiction_selected`'s last hypothesis by `m`\nis what makes `ν` comparable with the other three slacks: on its own `ν` is not small — it is\nbounded below by `hgap` — and only `ν/m` is. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.fin_of_slack","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.fin_of_slack","kind":"theorem","line":345,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothFinalAssembly.lean#L345-L369","doc":"**The four slacks, against `δ`.**  Step 1's shortfall `ε`, the polynomial's share `θ`, the\nnormalisation `η`, and Roth's `ν` measured against `m`, all below `t`, with `t(16+5δ) < δ`.\n\nOnly the *upper* bounds are needed.  I wrote `0 ≤ ε`, `0 ≤ η`, `0 ≤ νm` and `t ≤ 1` as well and the\nlinter reported all four unused, which is right: a negative slack is slack to spare on both sides,\nand `t < 1/5` already follows from `t(16+5δ) < δ` with `t, δ > 0`.  The stronger statement is\nkept. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.roth_contradiction_selected","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.roth_contradiction_selected","kind":"theorem","line":140,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothFinalAssembly.lean#L140-L321","doc":"**Steps 1 → 2 → 3 → 4, with the parameters chosen rather than assumed.**\n\n`roth_contradiction_int` needed nine facts about data it did not produce — the degrees `r`, the\nheights `H`, the auxiliary polynomial's height bound, the approximations.  Here every one of them\nis produced, and what is left is a list of conditions on constants alone:\n\n* `hfloorExp`, `hgap`, `hpq` — the ladder's own arithmetic, unchanged from `Γ_m`;\n* `hm2 : 2·[K:ℚ] < 4ε²m` — unknowns beat conditions, with the factor 2 that keeps Siegel's\n  exponent at most 1;\n* `hT` — one floor on the heights, discharging *both* the height clause (`hTsiegel`) and the\n  polynomial's share of the denominators (`hTtheta`);\n* `hfin` — Roth's inequality, with `log N` already divided out.\n\n`hfin` is the interesting one.  In `(1+θ)·m·(1+η) < (2+δ)(1−η)·(m(½−ε) − ν)` the normalisation\nslack `η = 1/k` appears on both sides with opposite signs: it inflates the denominators' total\nsize and deflates the approximation exponent.  Writing `2 + δ' := (2+δ)(1−η)` turns it into\nexactly `exists_m_of_slack`'s shape at `δ'`, so **the excess the slack budget is paid from is not\n`δ` but `δ − η(2+δ)`** — the approximation quality net of the normalisation.  That is the honest\nstatement of \"choose `N` large compared with the heights\", and it is why `k` had to become a\nparameter of the selection.\n\nThe supply `app` is the negation of Roth's theorem in the form the proof consumes: for every\nheight in an unbounded set `S` there is a rational of *exactly* that height approximating `ψ α₀` to\nwithin `H^{-(2+δ)}`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.hpt_of_floor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.hpt_of_floor","kind":"theorem","line":107,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothFinalAssembly.lean#L107-L130","doc":"**`hpt` from a floor on the heights.**  `roth_contradiction_of_normalisation`'s `hpt` clause is\n`2 + cH + log(2B) + log 2 ≤ θ·log q_h`.  Routed through `height_le_mul_den`, it becomes a condition\non a *number* `T` below every height — and `T` is precisely the selection's floor parameter, so this\nclause costs nothing beyond enlarging `T`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.height_le_mul_den","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.height_le_mul_den","kind":"theorem","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothFinalAssembly.lean#L83-L105","doc":"**A bounded rational's height is at most `B` times its denominator.**  `H = max(|num|, den)`,\nand `|num| ≤ B·den` is just `|κ| ≤ B` cleared of its denominator; `den ≤ B·den` needs `B ≥ 1`.\n\nThis is the lemma that lets a floor on the *heights* — which is all the selection can impose,\nsince `S` is a set of heights — serve as a floor on the *denominators*, which is what `hpt`\nactually constrains.  Without it the two floors are unrelated: a rational of height `10^9` can\nhave denominator `1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.selection_normalisation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.selection_normalisation","kind":"theorem","line":57,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothFinalAssembly.lean#L57-L77","doc":"**The normalisation slack is `1/k`.**  `rothDegrees_log_bounds` is two-sided to within\n`log H_h`, and the selection's `(H h)^k ≤ N` bounds that by `(1/k)·log N`.  So the degrees satisfy\n`(1 − η)·log N ≤ r_h·log H_h ≤ (1 + η)·log N` with `η = 1/k`, uniformly in `h` — which is what\n`hT` and `hL'` need, and neither can get from a one-sided estimate. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.log_pow_le_log","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.log_pow_le_log","kind":"theorem","line":47,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothFinalAssembly.lean#L47-L55","doc":"`H^k ≤ N`, in logs.  The selection's new clause, made usable. ","signatureChunk":0}]}